## Maintainer verification (2026-10-08)

Integrated current main `24c3201e57c` because the managed-worktree documentation changed upstream. Candidate: `7126fe4ed58e3366e21403e1b44c561207e30c99`.

A fresh built-Gateway Docker run reproduced the issue on main `cd8b5d37d591fa27ac33e592d0dad65123e8f79e`: after dispatch at A, advancing the local repository to B and re-specifying the card to B, a second worker started without a warning while checkout HEAD remained A. The same shipped CLI/Gateway flow on the candidate returned no started worker and an explicit A/B base-ref mismatch. Modified README.md and untracked draft.txt contents survived. Restoring the card's requested base to A successfully dispatched again in the retained dirty checkout. Model replies were supplied by a local synthetic OpenAI-compatible server; dispatch and worktree operations used production paths.

`pnpm build:docker` and real `pnpm tsgo` passed in isolation. A separate exact-head live agent call to gpt-5-mini passed with low reasoning and a 4096-token output budget.

No overlap with Pash/Sarah changes.

This is a two-way door: reverting restores prior dispatch behavior; no schema or stored-data format changes. Blast radius is retained Workboard-owned worktree reuse with a changed explicit base ref. Same-ref reuse intentionally does not refresh a moving branch.

The contributor's earlier evidence below remains historical evidence; the new head requires its own CI and ClawSweeper result.

Closes #164786

## What Problem This Solves

Re-dispatching a Workboard card after changing `workspace.branch` could silently start its worker in the retained checkout at the original base.

## User Impact

Workboard dispatch now reports both the retained and requested base refs before starting a worker. The existing checkout and local work are preserved, including when the checkout is clean. Restore the original base ref to continue the card, or create a new card for a different base. Reusing the same ref or omitting it keeps the existing checkout without refreshing it when a branch advances.

## Why This Change Was Made

The existing owner-reuse entry point checks repository identity but ignores the requested base. It now applies the new base-ref refusal **only to retained Workboard-owned records**. Keeping this check in the owner implementation preserves its existing repository validation, lifecycle, and authority checks before a retained checkout is returned.

Manual, default-manual, and session callers of the shared worktree service retain their previous reuse behavior, including requests with equivalent ref names or a later commit. Gateway session preparation retains its separate, existing selection validation. Named snapshot restoration is unchanged. No public API, configuration, or stored-data format is added. The Workboard tool description and managed-worktree guide document the card's reuse lifecycle.

## Evidence

**Real Gateway before/after (`ce871c273c` vs. its upstream `main` parent `6dd7b2e00b`)**

The current head `718b173672` only merges newer `main`. This PR's five files are identical to `ce871c273c`, so this proof still applies.

Both runs use the same script against a running Gateway and differ only in this PR's five files. The source checkout was installed with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and launched with `pnpm openclaw gateway run`, using isolated state, config, and `HOME`, loopback token auth, and channels skipped. Every Workboard step goes through the shipped CLI (`openclaw workboard dispatch|move|show`, `openclaw worktrees list`) or `openclaw gateway call` to that Gateway. Dispatch, worktree materialization, and `subagent.run` are the production paths. Only the model replies come from the repository's QA mock provider (`pnpm openclaw qa mock-openai`). The fixture is a local Git repository with commits A and B.

The flow follows #164786:

1. Create a ready card with `workspace: { kind: "worktree", path: <repo>, branch: A }`.
2. Dispatch with `--max-starts 1 --admin`. The worker starts in `wb-<card>` at A.
3. Record completion of run 1 with `workboard.cards.complete`. The mock model does not call `workboard_complete`, so the operator method stands in for it; it is the same store operation, and it releases the claim.
4. Leave unfinished work in the retained checkout: a modified `README.md` and an untracked `draft.txt`.
5. Advance the source repository to B.
6. Move the card to `todo`, call `workboard.cards.specify` with `branch: B`, move it to `ready`, and dispatch again.

| Second dispatch (requests B) | `main` `6dd7b2e00b` | This PR `ce871c273c` |
|---|---|---|
| Dispatch result | one run in `started`, `startFailures: []`; no warning | `started: []`; `startFailures[0].error`: `worktree wb-… already uses base ref A; requested B. Existing checkout preserved; use a new owner and name for a different base.` |
| Worker runs (`workboard.cards.runs`) | 2: a second worker launched | 1: no second launch |
| Card afterwards | `review`, claimed by the new run; card records `sourceBranch: B` | `blocked`, claim released, with comment `Dispatcher could not start worker: …` |
| Checkout `HEAD` | A: the new worker ran on the old base | A |
| Local work (` M README.md`, `?? draft.txt`) | preserved | preserved |
| Registry row (`openclaw worktrees list`) | `baseRef: A` | `baseRef: A`, `removedAt: null` |

Recovery on this PR: after the card was re-specified to A, dispatch started a worker in the same checkout. `HEAD` stayed at A and `draft.txt` was still present. Gateway logs show the mock worker's tool calls resolving inside the retained checkout, confirming the run used it as its cwd. Paths below are redacted to `<proof>`, and claim tokens are redacted by the CLI.

